Microsoft Learn: Introduction to Azure Load Balancer

Platform: Microsoft Learn
Module: Introduction to Azure Load Balancer
Track: AZ-104 Microsoft Azure Administrator
Difficulty: Beginner
Date: 2026

Objective

Learn how Azure Load Balancer distributes network traffic across multiple Azure resources to improve availability, scalability, and fault tolerance.

This module focused on understanding Azure Load Balancer functionality, how load balancing works, and identifying scenarios where Azure Load Balancer is the appropriate solution for application delivery and high availability.

Step 1: Understanding Azure Load Balancer

The module introduced Azure Load Balancer as a Layer 4 (Transport Layer) load balancing service that distributes incoming and outgoing network traffic across multiple backend resources.

Azure Load Balancer operates at:

OSI Layer 4
TCP
UDP
Primary purpose:

Distribute traffic across multiple servers to prevent a single server from becoming overloaded.

Example:
Internet Users
        ↓
Azure Load Balancer
    ↓         ↓
 VM1         VM2

Traffic is automatically distributed between the virtual machines.

Benefits:
High availability
Fault tolerance
Scalability
Improved performance
Automatic traffic distribution

The module emphasized that Azure Load Balancer is designed for high-performance network traffic management.

Step 2: Understanding How Azure Load Balancer Works

The training covered the components used by Azure Load Balancer.

Frontend IP Configuration

The frontend receives incoming client traffic.

Example:

Public IP
20.50.100.10

Users connect to the frontend IP address.

Backend Pool

The backend pool contains resources that receive traffic.

Examples:

Virtual Machines
Virtual Machine Scale Sets
Availability Sets

Example:

Backend Pool

VM1
VM2
VM3

The Load Balancer distributes requests among these resources.

Health Probes

Health probes continuously check backend resource health.

Probe types:
Protocol	Purpose
TCP	Port availability
HTTP	Web application health
HTTPS	Secure application health

Example:

Check VM1 Port 80

If a VM becomes unavailable:

VM1 = Failed

Traffic only sent to:
VM2
VM3
Benefits:
Automatic failure detection
Improved reliability
High availability

The module emphasized that health probes determine whether a resource can receive traffic.

Load Balancing Rules

Load balancing rules determine how traffic is distributed.

Example:

Frontend Port: 80
Backend Port: 80
Protocol: TCP

Traffic arriving on the frontend is forwarded to backend resources.

Key concepts learned:
Rules define traffic behavior
Rules connect frontend and backend configurations
Multiple rules can be configured
Step 3: Types of Azure Load Balancer

The module introduced the two Azure Load Balancer SKUs.

Basic Load Balancer

Features:

Smaller workloads
Limited functionality
Legacy deployments
Standard Load Balancer

Features:

Recommended for production workloads
Improved scalability
Higher availability
Enhanced security
Zone redundancy support
Comparison:
Feature	Basic	Standard
Production Ready	Limited	Yes
Availability Zones	No	Yes
Security Features	Limited	Enhanced
Scalability	Lower	Higher
Key lessons learned:
Standard Load Balancer is recommended for most deployments
Basic SKU is primarily for legacy environments
Step 4: Public and Internal Load Balancers

The training covered the two deployment models.

Public Load Balancer

Receives internet traffic.

Example:

Internet
    ↓
Public Load Balancer
    ↓
Web Servers

Common use cases:

Public websites
APIs
Internet-facing applications
Internal Load Balancer

Receives private network traffic only.

Example:

Application Servers
        ↓
Internal Load Balancer
        ↓
Database Servers

Common use cases:

Internal applications
Backend services
Multi-tier architectures
Key concepts learned:
Public Load Balancers expose services externally
Internal Load Balancers support private communication
